Substack gives you a blog, comments, SEO, and share cards for free.

I rebuilt all of it from scratch.

Not because Substack is bad. Because I wanted to own every line. Write, publish, and manage everything from my terminal with Claude Code. No dashboard. No browser. Just code.

That decision almost cost me my entire site on day one.

Here's every feature I had to replace, every mistake I made, and the security hole that could have given a stranger full access to my GitHub.

## What I Was Actually Replacing

Before writing code, I listed what Substack actually gives you.

Blog with reading time and share buttons. Comment threads. SEO meta tags and structured data. Social share images. Image optimization. A clean reading experience.

That's a lot of free infrastructure. You can see why most writers just use it.

I wrote about [why I left WordPress](/blog/why-i-left-wordpress) in a separate post. Short version: I use Claude Code every day but never touched my WordPress dashboard. So I needed a stack I could manage from my terminal.

I didn't just open my editor and start typing. I started in Claude's conversation UI (not Claude Code) and talked through everything first. What pages I need. What the content schemas should look like. Design direction. Deployment config.

By the end of that conversation, I had a full product spec.

Then I took that spec into Claude Code, ran it in plan mode, and let it scaffold the entire project. Astro setup, Keystatic config, pages, components, SEO, Cloudflare deployment. All from one spec.

The basic scaffold came together fast. Getting everything production-ready took the rest of those two days.

![The build spec Claude generated. Every page, tool, and design decision mapped out before writing a single line of code.](../../assets/images/blog/claude-build-spec.jpg)

## Git Init First. Always.

If you're building with AI tools, this habit will save you.

Initialize a git repo the moment the project exists. Commit after every meaningful change. Not at the end. Not when it's "ready." From the very first scaffold.

Two reasons.

**Context.** Claude Code reads your git history. When it sees what you've already built, what files changed, what the commit messages say, it makes better decisions about what to do next. Your commit history is basically a conversation log between you and your project.

**Rollback.** Claude Code makes a change that breaks something? You're one `git checkout` away from the last working state. No panic. No guessing. Just revert and try again.

I committed locally after every feature, every fix, every refactor. Not to push anywhere. Just to save my progress. It costs nothing and it's saved me more than once.

## Cloning Substack's Blog Layout

If you're going to replace Substack, the reading experience has to be at least as good.

So I studied how Substack does it. Reading time at the top, comment count, share buttons. I took that structure and ran with it. Time to read, share icons (almost straight from Substack), and a table of contents on the side that scrolls with you and collapses when you click it.

**My site:**

![The aristotle.me blog layout. Byline, reading time, collapsible table of contents. Heavily inspired by Substack.](../../assets/images/blog/blog-layout-aristotle.png)

**Substack:**

![A typical Substack post for comparison. Same structure: title, subtitle, author row, engagement bar, sticky TOC on the left.](../../assets/images/blog/blog-layout-substack.png)

Side by side, they're close. That was the goal.

### Mobile Table of Contents (Substack Doesn't Have One)

Desktop table of contents works great. Sticky sidebar, collapse toggle, highlights the section you're reading. But on mobile, it disappears. For a post with 15 sections, that's a lot of blind scrolling. Substack doesn't solve this either.

I added a floating button in the bottom-right corner. Tap it, a bottom sheet slides up with every section listed. Tap one, smooth scroll, sheet closes. Small addition. Big difference in usability.

## Building My Own Comment System

Substack gives you threaded comments for free. I had to build mine.

I looked at open source options. Giscus requires a GitHub account to comment. Most of my readers aren't developers. Cusdis looked promising but their own site wouldn't even load. Not a great sign for a dependency.

So I built my own. Cloudflare D1 for storage, Turnstile for spam protection, honeypot fields, rate limiting, and threaded replies. Full control.

### The Reply UX I Got Wrong

First version: you click "Reply" on a comment, it scrolls you all the way back up to the main form at the top. Shows "Replying to [name]" but now you're at the top of the page. Away from the conversation you were reading.

Bad UX.

I rebuilt it. Now clicking "Reply" opens an inline form right below that comment. Name field, text field, submit button. You stay in context. You can see what you're replying to.

Much better.

Managing Turnstile's spam protection across two forms on the same page (the main form and inline reply form) was more frustrating than building the comment storage itself. It works. But it was the most annoying part of the entire build.

![The comment system. Turnstile widget, threaded replies, inline reply form. More work than it looks.](../../assets/images/blog/comment-system.png)

## The XSS I Almost Shipped

Remember the security hole I mentioned? Here's the full story.

The comment form takes a name and a message. I was putting that text directly into the page without cleaning it first. So if someone types code instead of their name, the browser treats it as part of the website and runs it.

The real risk was me. I use GitHub OAuth to manage this site. If I'm logged in and view a malicious comment, that script runs with my auth tokens. Someone could grab my GitHub token, push code to my repo, and since Cloudflare auto-deploys from GitHub, they'd own my entire site. Through my own comment section.

The fix took two minutes. But finding it took longer.

On Substack, someone else worries about this. When you own the stack, you catch these yourself. AI tools write code fast. They don't always write it safe.

## The Design Was Ugly (Until Playwright MCP Fixed It)

Substack at least gives you a decent default. My first version wasn't even that.

I installed [Playwright MCP](https://github.com/microsoft/playwright-mcp) into my Claude Code workflow. This lets Claude Code actually *see* the UI in a real browser. Not just the code. The actual rendered page.

I told it to go through the whole site and tell me what could be improved.

The initial design was plain. Functional but bland. Claude Code came back with suggestions. Warmer color palette, better card elevations, the white-and-cement color pattern you see now. The site looked significantly better after that one pass.

I also pulled two recommendations from LinkedIn and added them as testimonials. The original reviews were long. Nobody reads a full paragraph from a stranger. So I trimmed them to the strongest two sentences each.

## Things I Built and Then Deleted

**The resume page.** Built a full resume page, then killed it. Every job wants a customized resume now. A generic one on your website doesn't help anyone. LinkedIn already does that job. I replaced it with an About section instead.

**The pre-commit hook.** I was worried about accidentally publishing draft blog posts. So I built a git hook that scans staged files for `draft: true` and blocks the commit.

Then I realized the code already filters out drafts. They never show on the live site regardless. And if I ever use Keystatic from a browser, drafts need to be in the repo for the CMS to see them. The hook would break that.

Removed it two commits later.

Both times, the right move was deleting what I just built. That's harder than it sounds.

## The Words Mattered More Than the Code

This surprised me. After the whole build was done, I asked Claude Code to review the copy across the site. Not the code. The words.

My homepage hero said: *"I do marketing, analytics, AI, automation, and product dev. Basically anything that lets me avoid a boring day."*

Meanwhile my About page said: *"I see how things should work. Then I build them."*

See the gap?

The About page was carrying the whole site. The homepage, the first thing anyone reads, sounded like a LinkedIn headline.

I rewrote everything in one pass. Trimmed testimonials. De-duplicated taglines. Tightened every description.

Zero code changed. More impact than any technical feature I shipped.

## 3.1MB of Images on a "Fast" Site

After publishing, I checked the network tab. The blog images were loading as raw PNGs and JPGs. No compression. No responsive sizing. One screenshot was 1.4MB.

Substack handles image optimization automatically. I was serving raw screenshots like it was 2008.

Astro has a built-in image component that handles compression, format conversion, and responsive sizing automatically. I just wasn't using it.

I told Claude Code to fix this. It restructured how images flow through the build pipeline. Here's what happened to the file sizes:

| Image | Before | After (480w) | After (1024w) | Reduction |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| blog-layout-substack.png | 1,468kB | 18kB | 124kB | 99% |
| blog-layout-aristotle.png | 872kB | 19kB | 94kB | 98% |
| claude-build-spec.jpg | 568kB | 24kB | 71kB | 96% |
| comment-system.png | 264kB | 7kB | 24kB | 97% |
| **Total** | **3,172kB** | **68kB** | **313kB** | |

3.1MB down to 68kB on mobile. That 1.4MB Substack screenshot? 18kB.

The initial build is never done. The best improvements come from actually using your site and noticing what's slow.

## SEO for Google and AI Search

After fixing images, I compared my site to what Substack and WordPress give you out of the box. I was missing a lot. Not just traditional SEO. AI search too. ChatGPT, Perplexity, Claude. These tools are answering questions now, and they need to know your site exists.

The biggest gap on the traditional side: no social share images. Every time someone shared a post on Twitter or LinkedIn, it showed up as a plain text link. No preview card. No image. Just a URL.

Substack generates all of this automatically. On a custom stack, you build it yourself.

### OG Images That Don't Look Auto-Generated

I didn't want to design a share image every time I publish. So I built a system that generates them automatically at build time. Every post gets a branded 1200x630 PNG.

First version was bland. Blue rectangle, white text, "AM" circle in the corner. Looked like every other auto-generated share card on the internet.

So I iterated. Swapped in my pixel art avatar. Added the post description. Unified the branding.

**Before:**

![First version. Flat blue, generic AM circle, no description.](../../assets/images/blog/og-image-before.jpg)

**After:**

![Final version. Gradient background, pixel art avatar, post description, content anchored to the bottom.](../../assets/images/blog/og-image-after.png)

The difference between "auto-generated" and "intentional" is usually just a few design decisions.

### Structured Data, Meta Tags, and llms.txt

Beyond OG images, I added proper Twitter tags, structured data for Google's Knowledge Panels and breadcrumbs, and fixed the RSS feed. The share modal now shows the actual OG image instead of a placeholder.

Then I added an `llms.txt` file. It's a growing standard that tells AI models what your site is about and how it's structured. Think of it like `robots.txt` but for ChatGPT, Perplexity, and Claude instead of Google's crawler.

None of this changes how the site looks. But it's the difference between a bare URL and a branded card when someone shares your post. And the difference between AI search knowing your site exists or not.

## Perfect 100s on Lighthouse

After deploying, I ran Google Lighthouse on every page.

Not perfect on the first run. Blog posts scored 96 on accessibility and best practices. Contrast issues, heading order problems, console errors.

I told Claude Code to fix everything Lighthouse flagged. One pass. After redeploying, every page hit 100/100/100/100 on desktop. Performance, accessibility, best practices, SEO. All maxed out.

Substack doesn't make you think about Lighthouse scores. But when you share your site in a job application or a client pitch, those numbers say something. They say you care about the details, not just the features.

## The Numbers

About 2 days of actual build time, spread across 5 calendar days between other projects. Sub-500ms page loads on a global CDN. $0/month hosting. Perfect 100s on every Lighthouse category. Zero plugins beyond core Astro integrations.

The site is open source: [github.com/aristotlem/aristotle-me](https://github.com/aristotlem/aristotle-me)

---

Substack would have been easier. Sign up, write, publish. No deployment config, no CSS bugs at midnight.

But I own every line of this. The layout, the comments, the SEO, the share cards. No platform can change the rules on me. No pricing update can lock me out.

More work. More yours.
